window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2018-12-3", "source": "2018年12月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on how to balance academic study and extracurricular activities. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "University life offers not only lectures and exams but also clubs, sports and volunteer work. Yet many students\nfeel torn between the two, letting one crowd out the other. In my view, academic study and extracurricular\nactivities are not rivals; with a few practical habits, they can reinforce each other.\nThe first step is to make a weekly plan and prioritize tasks by importance. By listing deadlines and fixed\nactivities in one schedule, I can set aside blocks for study while reserving time for the clubs I love, so neither is\nneglected. Equally important is to raise my study efficiency. When I concentrate fully during class and finish\nassignments promptly, I save hours that would otherwise be wasted, leaving room for the basketball team\nwithout hurting my grades.\nIn short, balancing the two is far from impossible. Once we plan ahead, sort out what matters most and study\nmore efficiently, academic work and campus activities can grow together rather than at each other's expense."}, "translation": {"prompt": "近年来，中国政府进一步加大体育馆建设投资，以更好地满足人们快速增长的健身需求。除了新建体育馆外，许多城市换采取了改造旧工厂和商业建筑等措施，来增加当地体育馆的数量。在政府资金的支持下，越来越多的体育馆向公众免费开放，或者只收取少量费用。许多体育馆通过应用现代信息技术大大提高了服务质量。人民可以方便地在线预订场地和付费。可以预见，随着运动设施的不断改善，愈来愈多的人将会去体育馆健身。", "sample": "In recent years, the Chinese government has further increased its investment in the construction of gymnasiums to better meet the rapidly growing fitness needs of the people. In addition to building new gymnasiums, many cities have also taken measures such as renovating old factories and commercial buildings to increase the number of local gymnasiums. With the support of government funding, more and more gymnasiums are open to the public for free, or charge only a small fee. Many gymnasiums have greatly improved their service quality by applying modern information technology. People can conveniently book venues and make payments online. It can be predicted that with the continuous improvement of sports facilities, more and more people will go to gymnasiums to work out."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "In what's probably the craziest headline I've ever written, I've reported that  __26__  in livestock protection are happening with scientists painting eyes on the butts of cows. The experiment is based upon the idea that farmers who're protecting their herd from lions would shoot and kill lions in an effort to protect their livestock. While this makes a lot of sense, it results in many lion deaths that  __27__  would have been unnecessary. Researchers in Australia have been  __28__  and testing a method of trickery to make lions think they are being watched by the painted eyes on cow butts.\nThis idea is based on the principle that lions and other  __29__  are far less likely to attack when they feel they are being watched. As conservation areas become smaller, lions are increasingly coming into contact with human populations, which are expanding to the  __30__  of these protected areas.\nEfforts like painting eyes on cow butts may seem crazy at first, but they could make actual headway in the fight for conservation. \"If the method works, it could provide farmers in Botswana—and  __31__  —with a low-cost, sustainable tool to protect their livestock, and a way to keep lions safe from being killed.\"\nLions are  __32__  ambush(埋伏) hunters, so when they feel their prey has  __33__  them, they usually give up on the hunt. Researchers are  __34__  testing their idea on a select herd of cattle. They have painted half of the cows with eyes and left the other half as normal. Through satellite tracking of both the herd and the lions in the area, they will be able to  __35__  if their psychological trickery will work to help keep farmers from shooting lions.", "matchIntro": "Resilience Is About How You Recharge, Not How You Endure", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "As constant travelers and parents of a 2-year-old, we sometimes fantasize about how much work we can do when one of us gets on a plane, undistracted by phones, friends, or movies. We race to get all our ground work done: packing, going through security, doing a last-minute work call, calling each other, then boarding the plane. Then, when we try to have that amazing work session in flight, we get nothing done. Even worse, after refreshing our email or reading the same studies over and over, we are too exhausted when we land to soldier on with(继续处理) the emails that have inevitably still piled up."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Why should flying deplete us? We're just sitting there doing nothing. Why can't we be tougher, more resilient(有复原力的) and determined in our work so we can accomplish all of the goals we set for ourselves? Based on our current research, we have come to realize that the problem is not our hectic schedule or the plane travel itself; the problem comes from a misconception of what it means to be resilient, and the resulting impact of overworking."}, {"label": "C", "text": "We often take a militaristic, \"tough\" approach to resilience and determination like a Marine pulling himself through the mud, a boxer going one more round, or a football player picking himself up off the ground for one more play. We believe that the longer we tough it out, the tougher we are, and therefore the more successful we will be. However, this entire conception is scientifically inaccurate."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The very lack of a recovery period is dramatically holding back our collective ability to be resilient and successful. Research has found that there is a direct correlation between lack of recovery and increased incidence of health and safety problems. And lack of recovery—whether by disrupting sleep with thoughts of work or having continuous cognitive arousal by watching our phones—is costing our companies $62 billion a year in lost productivity."}, {"label": "E", "text": "And just because work stops, it doesn't mean we are recovering. We \"stop\" work sometimes at 5 pm, but then we spend the night wrestling with solutions to work problems, talking about our work over dinner, and falling asleep thinking about how much work we'll do tomorrow. In a study just released, researchers from Norway found that 7.8% of Norwegians have become workaholics(工作狂). The scientists cite a definition of \"workaholism\" as \"being overly concerned about work, driven by an uncontrollable work motivation, and investing so much time and effort in work that it impairs other important life areas.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "We believe that the number of people who fit that definition includes the majority of American workers, which prompted us to begin a study of workaholism in the U.S. Our study will use a large corporate dataset from a major medical company to examine how technology extends our working hours and thus interferes with necessary cognitive recovery, resulting in huge health care costs and turnover costs for employers."}, {"label": "G", "text": "The misconception of resilience is often bred from an early age. Parents trying to teach their children resilience might celebrate a high school student staying up until 3 am to finish a science fair project. What a distortion of resilience! A resilient child is a well-rested one. When an exhausted student goes to school, he risks hurting everyone on the road with his impaired driving; he doesn't have the cognitive resources to do well on his English test; he has lower self-control with his friends; and at home, he is moody with his parents. Overwork and exhaustion are the opposite of resilience and the bad habits we acquire when we're young only magnify when we hit the workforce."}, {"label": "H", "text": "As Jim Loehr and Tony Schwartz have written, if you have too much time in the performance zone, you need more time in the recovery zone, otherwise you risk burnout. Gathering your resources to \"try hard\" requires burning energy in order to overcome your currently low arousal level. It also worsens exhaustion. Thus the more imbalanced we become due to overworking, the more value there is in activities that allow us to return to a state of balance. The value of a recovery period rises in proportion to the amount of work required of us."}, {"label": "I", "text": "So how do we recover and build resilience? Most people assume that if you stop doing a task like answering emails or writing a paper, your brain will naturally recover, so that when you start again later in the day or the next morning, you'll have your energy back. But surely everyone reading this has had times when you lie in bed for hours, unable to fall asleep because your brain is thinking about work. If you lie in bed for eight hours, you may have rested, but you can still feel exhausted the next day. That's because rest and recovery are not the same thing."}, {"label": "J", "text": "If you're trying to build resilience at work, you need adequate internal and external recovery periods. As researchers Zijlstra, Cropley and Rydstedt write in their 2014 paper: \"Internal recovery refers to the shorter periods of relaxation that take place within the frames of the work day or the work setting in the form of short scheduled or unscheduled breaks, by shifting attention or changing to other work tasks when the mental or physical resources required for the initial task are temporarily depleted or exhausted. External recovery refers to actions that take place outside of work—e.g. in the free time between the work days, and during weekends, holidays or vacations.\" If after work you lie around on your bed and get irritated by political commentary on your phone or get stressed thinking about decisions about how to renovate your home, your brain has not received a break from high mental arousal states. Our brains need a rest as much as our bodies do."}, {"label": "K", "text": "If you really want to build resilience, you can start by strategically stopping. Give yourself the resources to be tough by creating internal and external recovery periods. Amy Blankson describes how to strategically stop during the day by using technology to control overworking. She suggests downloading the Instant or Moment apps to see how many times you turn on your phone each day. You can also use apps like Offtime or Unplugged to create tech free zones by strategically scheduling automatic airplane modes. The average person turns on their phone 150 times every day. If every distraction took only 1 minute, that would account for 2.5 hours a day."}, {"label": "L", "text": "In addition, you can take a cognitive break every 90 minutes to charge your batteries. Try to not have lunch at your desk, but instead spend time outside or with your friends—not talking about work. Take all of your paid time off, which not only gives you recovery periods, but raises your productivity and likelihood of promotion."}, {"label": "M", "text": "As for us, we've started using our plane time as a work-free zone, and thus time to dip into the recovery phase. The results have been fantastic. We are usually tired already by the time we get on a plane, and the crowded space and unstable internet connection make work more challenging. Now, instead of swimming upstream, we relax, sleep, watch movies, or listen to music. And when we get off the plane, instead of being depleted, we feel recovered and ready to return to the performance zone."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Children with attention problems in early childhood were 40% less likely to graduate from high school, says a new study from Duke University.\nThe study included 386 kindergarteners from schools in the Fast Track Project, a multi-site clinical trial in the U.S. that in 1991 began tracking how children developed across their lives.\nWith this study, researchers examined early academic attention and socio-emotional skills and how each contributed to academic success into young adulthood.\nThey found that early attention skills were the most consistent predictor of academic success, and that likability by peers also had a modest effect on academic performance.\nBy fifth grade, children with early attention difficulties had lower grades and reading achievement scores than their peers. As fifth-graders, children with early attention problems obtained average reading scores at least 3% lower than their contemporaries' and grades at least 8% lower than those of their peers. This was after controlling for IQ, socio-economic status and academic skills at school entry.\nAlthough these may not seem like large effects, the impact of early attention problems continued throughout the children's academic careers. Lower reading achievement scores and grades in fifth grade contributed to reduced grades in middle school and thereby contributed to a 40% lower high school graduation rate.\n\"The children we identified as having attention difficulties were not diagnosed with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder(注意力缺乏多动症)(ADHD), although some may have had the disorder. Our findings suggest that even more modest attention difficulties can increase the risk of negative academic outcomes,\" said David Rabiner, an associate dean of Duke's Trinity College of Arts & Sciences, whose research has focused on ADHD and interventions to improve academic performance in children with attention difficulties.\nSocial acceptance by peers in early childhood also predicted grades in fifth grade. Children not as liked by their first-grade peers had slightly lower grades in fifth grade, while those with higher social acceptance had higher grades.\n\"This study shows the importance of so-called 'non-cognitive' or soft skills in contributing to children's positive peer relationships, which, in turn, contribute to their academic success,\" said Kenneth Dodge, director of the Duke Center for Child and Family Policy.\nThe results highlight the need to develop effective early interventions to help those with attention problems stay on track academically and for educators to encourage positive peer relationships, the researchers said.\n\"We're learning that student success requires a more comprehensive approach, one that incorporates not only academic skills but also social, self-regulatory and attention skills,\" Dodge said. \"If we neglect any of these areas, the child's development lags. If we attend to these areas, a child's success may reinforce itself with positive feedback loops.\"", "47": "Children with attention problems in early childhood were 40% less likely to graduate from high school, says a new study from Duke University.\nThe study included 386 kindergarteners from schools in the Fast Track Project, a multi-site clinical trial in the U.S. that in 1991 began tracking how children developed across their lives.\nWith this study, researchers examined early academic attention and socio-emotional skills and how each contributed to academic success into young adulthood.\nThey found that early attention skills were the most consistent predictor of academic success, and that likability by peers also had a modest effect on academic performance.\nBy fifth grade, children with early attention difficulties had lower grades and reading achievement scores than their peers. As fifth-graders, children with early attention problems obtained average reading scores at least 3% lower than their contemporaries' and grades at least 8% lower than those of their peers. This was after controlling for IQ, socio-economic status and academic skills at school entry.\nAlthough these may not seem like large effects, the impact of early attention problems continued throughout the children's academic careers. Lower reading achievement scores and grades in fifth grade contributed to reduced grades in middle school and thereby contributed to a 40% lower high school graduation rate.\n\"The children we identified as having attention difficulties were not diagnosed with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder(注意力缺乏多动症)(ADHD), although some may have had the disorder. Our findings suggest that even more modest attention difficulties can increase the risk of negative academic outcomes,\" said David Rabiner, an associate dean of Duke's Trinity College of Arts & Sciences, whose research has focused on ADHD and interventions to improve academic performance in children with attention difficulties.\nSocial acceptance by peers in early childhood also predicted grades in fifth grade. Children not as liked by their first-grade peers had slightly lower grades in fifth grade, while those with higher social acceptance had higher grades.\n\"This study shows the importance of so-called 'non-cognitive' or soft skills in contributing to children's positive peer relationships, which, in turn, contribute to their academic success,\" said Kenneth Dodge, director of the Duke Center for Child and Family Policy.\nThe results highlight the need to develop effective early interventions to help those with attention problems stay on track academically and for educators to encourage positive peer relationships, the researchers said.\n\"We're learning that student success requires a more comprehensive approach, one that incorporates not only academic skills but also social, self-regulatory and attention skills,\" Dodge said. \"If we neglect any of these areas, the child's development lags. If we attend to these areas, a child's success may reinforce itself with positive feedback loops.\"", "48": "Children with attention problems in early childhood were 40% less likely to graduate from high school, says a new study from Duke University.\nThe study included 386 kindergarteners from schools in the Fast Track Project, a multi-site clinical trial in the U.S. that in 1991 began tracking how children developed across their lives.\nWith this study, researchers examined early academic attention and socio-emotional skills and how each contributed to academic success into young adulthood.\nThey found that early attention skills were the most consistent predictor of academic success, and that likability by peers also had a modest effect on academic performance.\nBy fifth grade, children with early attention difficulties had lower grades and reading achievement scores than their peers. As fifth-graders, children with early attention problems obtained average reading scores at least 3% lower than their contemporaries' and grades at least 8% lower than those of their peers. This was after controlling for IQ, socio-economic status and academic skills at school entry.\nAlthough these may not seem like large effects, the impact of early attention problems continued throughout the children's academic careers. Lower reading achievement scores and grades in fifth grade contributed to reduced grades in middle school and thereby contributed to a 40% lower high school graduation rate.\n\"The children we identified as having attention difficulties were not diagnosed with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder(注意力缺乏多动症)(ADHD), although some may have had the disorder. Our findings suggest that even more modest attention difficulties can increase the risk of negative academic outcomes,\" said David Rabiner, an associate dean of Duke's Trinity College of Arts & Sciences, whose research has focused on ADHD and interventions to improve academic performance in children with attention difficulties.\nSocial acceptance by peers in early childhood also predicted grades in fifth grade. Children not as liked by their first-grade peers had slightly lower grades in fifth grade, while those with higher social acceptance had higher grades.\n\"This study shows the importance of so-called 'non-cognitive' or soft skills in contributing to children's positive peer relationships, which, in turn, contribute to their academic success,\" said Kenneth Dodge, director of the Duke Center for Child and Family Policy.\nThe results highlight the need to develop effective early interventions to help those with attention problems stay on track academically and for educators to encourage positive peer relationships, the researchers said.\n\"We're learning that student success requires a more comprehensive approach, one that incorporates not only academic skills but also social, self-regulatory and attention skills,\" Dodge said. \"If we neglect any of these areas, the child's development lags. If we attend to these areas, a child's success may reinforce itself with positive feedback loops.\"", "49": "Children with attention problems in early childhood were 40% less likely to graduate from high school, says a new study from Duke University.\nThe study included 386 kindergarteners from schools in the Fast Track Project, a multi-site clinical trial in the U.S. that in 1991 began tracking how children developed across their lives.\nWith this study, researchers examined early academic attention and socio-emotional skills and how each contributed to academic success into young adulthood.\nThey found that early attention skills were the most consistent predictor of academic success, and that likability by peers also had a modest effect on academic performance.\nBy fifth grade, children with early attention difficulties had lower grades and reading achievement scores than their peers. As fifth-graders, children with early attention problems obtained average reading scores at least 3% lower than their contemporaries' and grades at least 8% lower than those of their peers. This was after controlling for IQ, socio-economic status and academic skills at school entry.\nAlthough these may not seem like large effects, the impact of early attention problems continued throughout the children's academic careers. Lower reading achievement scores and grades in fifth grade contributed to reduced grades in middle school and thereby contributed to a 40% lower high school graduation rate.\n\"The children we identified as having attention difficulties were not diagnosed with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder(注意力缺乏多动症)(ADHD), although some may have had the disorder. Our findings suggest that even more modest attention difficulties can increase the risk of negative academic outcomes,\" said David Rabiner, an associate dean of Duke's Trinity College of Arts & Sciences, whose research has focused on ADHD and interventions to improve academic performance in children with attention difficulties.\nSocial acceptance by peers in early childhood also predicted grades in fifth grade. Children not as liked by their first-grade peers had slightly lower grades in fifth grade, while those with higher social acceptance had higher grades.\n\"This study shows the importance of so-called 'non-cognitive' or soft skills in contributing to children's positive peer relationships, which, in turn, contribute to their academic success,\" said Kenneth Dodge, director of the Duke Center for Child and Family Policy.\nThe results highlight the need to develop effective early interventions to help those with attention problems stay on track academically and for educators to encourage positive peer relationships, the researchers said.\n\"We're learning that student success requires a more comprehensive approach, one that incorporates not only academic skills but also social, self-regulatory and attention skills,\" Dodge said. \"If we neglect any of these areas, the child's development lags. If we attend to these areas, a child's success may reinforce itself with positive feedback loops.\"", "50": "Children with attention problems in early childhood were 40% less likely to graduate from high school, says a new study from Duke University.\nThe study included 386 kindergarteners from schools in the Fast Track Project, a multi-site clinical trial in the U.S. that in 1991 began tracking how children developed across their lives.\nWith this study, researchers examined early academic attention and socio-emotional skills and how each contributed to academic success into young adulthood.\nThey found that early attention skills were the most consistent predictor of academic success, and that likability by peers also had a modest effect on academic performance.\nBy fifth grade, children with early attention difficulties had lower grades and reading achievement scores than their peers. As fifth-graders, children with early attention problems obtained average reading scores at least 3% lower than their contemporaries' and grades at least 8% lower than those of their peers. This was after controlling for IQ, socio-economic status and academic skills at school entry.\nAlthough these may not seem like large effects, the impact of early attention problems continued throughout the children's academic careers. Lower reading achievement scores and grades in fifth grade contributed to reduced grades in middle school and thereby contributed to a 40% lower high school graduation rate.\n\"The children we identified as having attention difficulties were not diagnosed with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder(注意力缺乏多动症)(ADHD), although some may have had the disorder. Our findings suggest that even more modest attention difficulties can increase the risk of negative academic outcomes,\" said David Rabiner, an associate dean of Duke's Trinity College of Arts & Sciences, whose research has focused on ADHD and interventions to improve academic performance in children with attention difficulties.\nSocial acceptance by peers in early childhood also predicted grades in fifth grade. Children not as liked by their first-grade peers had slightly lower grades in fifth grade, while those with higher social acceptance had higher grades.\n\"This study shows the importance of so-called 'non-cognitive' or soft skills in contributing to children's positive peer relationships, which, in turn, contribute to their academic success,\" said Kenneth Dodge, director of the Duke Center for Child and Family Policy.\nThe results highlight the need to develop effective early interventions to help those with attention problems stay on track academically and for educators to encourage positive peer relationships, the researchers said.\n\"We're learning that student success requires a more comprehensive approach, one that incorporates not only academic skills but also social, self-regulatory and attention skills,\" Dodge said. \"If we neglect any of these areas, the child's development lags. If we attend to these areas, a child's success may reinforce itself with positive feedback loops.\"", "51": "On Jan. 9, 2007, Steve Jobs formally announced Apple's \"revolutionary mobile phone\"—a device that combined the functionality of an iPod, phone and Internet communication into a single unit, navigated by touch.\nIt was a huge milestone in the development of smartphones, which are now owned by a majority of American adults and are increasingly common across the globe.\nAs smartphones have multiplied, so have questions about their impact on how we live and how we work. Often the advantages of convenient, mobile technology are both obvious and taken for granted, leaving more subtle topics for concerned discussion: Are smartphones disturbing children's sleep? Is an inability to get away from work having a negative impact on health? And what are the implications for privacy?\nBut today, on the 10th anniversary of the iPhone, let's take a moment to consider a less obvious advantage: the potential for smartphone technology to revolutionize behavioral science. That's because, for the first time in human history, a large proportion of the species is in continuous contact with technology that can record key features of an individual's behavior and environment.\nResearchers have already begun to use smartphones in social scientific research, either to query people regularly as they engage in their normal lives or to record activity using the device's built-in sensors. These studies are confirming, challenging and extending what's been found using more traditional approaches, in which people report how they behaved in real life or participate in relatively short and artificial laboratory-based tasks.\nSuch studies are just first steps. As more data are collected and methods for analysis improve, researchers will be in a better position to identify how different experiences, behaviors and environments relate to each other and evolve over time, with the potential to improve people's productivity and wellbeing in a variety of domains. Beyond revealing population-wide patterns, the right combination of data and analysis can also help individuals identify unique characteristics of their own behavior, including conditions that could indicate the need for some form of intervention—such as an unusual increase in behaviors that signal a period of depression.\nSmartphone-based data collection comes at an appropriate time in the evolution of psychological science. Today, the field is in transition, moving away from a focus on laboratory studies with undergraduate participants towards more complex, real-world situations studied with more diverse groups of people. Smartphones offer new tools for achieving these ambitions, providing rich data about everyday behaviors in a variety of contexts.\nSo here's another way in which smartphones might transform the way we live and work: by offering insights into human psychology and behavior and, thus, supporting smarter social science.", "52": "On Jan. 9, 2007, Steve Jobs formally announced Apple's \"revolutionary mobile phone\"—a device that combined the functionality of an iPod, phone and Internet communication into a single unit, navigated by touch.\nIt was a huge milestone in the development of smartphones, which are now owned by a majority of American adults and are increasingly common across the globe.\nAs smartphones have multiplied, so have questions about their impact on how we live and how we work. Often the advantages of convenient, mobile technology are both obvious and taken for granted, leaving more subtle topics for concerned discussion: Are smartphones disturbing children's sleep? Is an inability to get away from work having a negative impact on health? And what are the implications for privacy?\nBut today, on the 10th anniversary of the iPhone, let's take a moment to consider a less obvious advantage: the potential for smartphone technology to revolutionize behavioral science. That's because, for the first time in human history, a large proportion of the species is in continuous contact with technology that can record key features of an individual's behavior and environment.\nResearchers have already begun to use smartphones in social scientific research, either to query people regularly as they engage in their normal lives or to record activity using the device's built-in sensors. These studies are confirming, challenging and extending what's been found using more traditional approaches, in which people report how they behaved in real life or participate in relatively short and artificial laboratory-based tasks.\nSuch studies are just first steps. As more data are collected and methods for analysis improve, researchers will be in a better position to identify how different experiences, behaviors and environments relate to each other and evolve over time, with the potential to improve people's productivity and wellbeing in a variety of domains. Beyond revealing population-wide patterns, the right combination of data and analysis can also help individuals identify unique characteristics of their own behavior, including conditions that could indicate the need for some form of intervention—such as an unusual increase in behaviors that signal a period of depression.\nSmartphone-based data collection comes at an appropriate time in the evolution of psychological science. Today, the field is in transition, moving away from a focus on laboratory studies with undergraduate participants towards more complex, real-world situations studied with more diverse groups of people. Smartphones offer new tools for achieving these ambitions, providing rich data about everyday behaviors in a variety of contexts.\nSo here's another way in which smartphones might transform the way we live and work: by offering insights into human psychology and behavior and, thus, supporting smarter social science.", "53": "On Jan. 9, 2007, Steve Jobs formally announced Apple's \"revolutionary mobile phone\"—a device that combined the functionality of an iPod, phone and Internet communication into a single unit, navigated by touch.\nIt was a huge milestone in the development of smartphones, which are now owned by a majority of American adults and are increasingly common across the globe.\nAs smartphones have multiplied, so have questions about their impact on how we live and how we work. Often the advantages of convenient, mobile technology are both obvious and taken for granted, leaving more subtle topics for concerned discussion: Are smartphones disturbing children's sleep? Is an inability to get away from work having a negative impact on health? And what are the implications for privacy?\nBut today, on the 10th anniversary of the iPhone, let's take a moment to consider a less obvious advantage: the potential for smartphone technology to revolutionize behavioral science. That's because, for the first time in human history, a large proportion of the species is in continuous contact with technology that can record key features of an individual's behavior and environment.\nResearchers have already begun to use smartphones in social scientific research, either to query people regularly as they engage in their normal lives or to record activity using the device's built-in sensors. These studies are confirming, challenging and extending what's been found using more traditional approaches, in which people report how they behaved in real life or participate in relatively short and artificial laboratory-based tasks.\nSuch studies are just first steps. As more data are collected and methods for analysis improve, researchers will be in a better position to identify how different experiences, behaviors and environments relate to each other and evolve over time, with the potential to improve people's productivity and wellbeing in a variety of domains. Beyond revealing population-wide patterns, the right combination of data and analysis can also help individuals identify unique characteristics of their own behavior, including conditions that could indicate the need for some form of intervention—such as an unusual increase in behaviors that signal a period of depression.\nSmartphone-based data collection comes at an appropriate time in the evolution of psychological science. Today, the field is in transition, moving away from a focus on laboratory studies with undergraduate participants towards more complex, real-world situations studied with more diverse groups of people. Smartphones offer new tools for achieving these ambitions, providing rich data about everyday behaviors in a variety of contexts.\nSo here's another way in which smartphones might transform the way we live and work: by offering insights into human psychology and behavior and, thus, supporting smarter social science.", "54": "On Jan. 9, 2007, Steve Jobs formally announced Apple's \"revolutionary mobile phone\"—a device that combined the functionality of an iPod, phone and Internet communication into a single unit, navigated by touch.\nIt was a huge milestone in the development of smartphones, which are now owned by a majority of American adults and are increasingly common across the globe.\nAs smartphones have multiplied, so have questions about their impact on how we live and how we work. Often the advantages of convenient, mobile technology are both obvious and taken for granted, leaving more subtle topics for concerned discussion: Are smartphones disturbing children's sleep? Is an inability to get away from work having a negative impact on health? And what are the implications for privacy?\nBut today, on the 10th anniversary of the iPhone, let's take a moment to consider a less obvious advantage: the potential for smartphone technology to revolutionize behavioral science. That's because, for the first time in human history, a large proportion of the species is in continuous contact with technology that can record key features of an individual's behavior and environment.\nResearchers have already begun to use smartphones in social scientific research, either to query people regularly as they engage in their normal lives or to record activity using the device's built-in sensors. These studies are confirming, challenging and extending what's been found using more traditional approaches, in which people report how they behaved in real life or participate in relatively short and artificial laboratory-based tasks.\nSuch studies are just first steps. As more data are collected and methods for analysis improve, researchers will be in a better position to identify how different experiences, behaviors and environments relate to each other and evolve over time, with the potential to improve people's productivity and wellbeing in a variety of domains. Beyond revealing population-wide patterns, the right combination of data and analysis can also help individuals identify unique characteristics of their own behavior, including conditions that could indicate the need for some form of intervention—such as an unusual increase in behaviors that signal a period of depression.\nSmartphone-based data collection comes at an appropriate time in the evolution of psychological science. Today, the field is in transition, moving away from a focus on laboratory studies with undergraduate participants towards more complex, real-world situations studied with more diverse groups of people. Smartphones offer new tools for achieving these ambitions, providing rich data about everyday behaviors in a variety of contexts.\nSo here's another way in which smartphones might transform the way we live and work: by offering insights into human psychology and behavior and, thus, supporting smarter social science.", "55": "On Jan. 9, 2007, Steve Jobs formally announced Apple's \"revolutionary mobile phone\"—a device that combined the functionality of an iPod, phone and Internet communication into a single unit, navigated by touch.\nIt was a huge milestone in the development of smartphones, which are now owned by a majority of American adults and are increasingly common across the globe.\nAs smartphones have multiplied, so have questions about their impact on how we live and how we work. Often the advantages of convenient, mobile technology are both obvious and taken for granted, leaving more subtle topics for concerned discussion: Are smartphones disturbing children's sleep? Is an inability to get away from work having a negative impact on health? And what are the implications for privacy?\nBut today, on the 10th anniversary of the iPhone, let's take a moment to consider a less obvious advantage: the potential for smartphone technology to revolutionize behavioral science. That's because, for the first time in human history, a large proportion of the species is in continuous contact with technology that can record key features of an individual's behavior and environment.\nResearchers have already begun to use smartphones in social scientific research, either to query people regularly as they engage in their normal lives or to record activity using the device's built-in sensors. These studies are confirming, challenging and extending what's been found using more traditional approaches, in which people report how they behaved in real life or participate in relatively short and artificial laboratory-based tasks.\nSuch studies are just first steps. As more data are collected and methods for analysis improve, researchers will be in a better position to identify how different experiences, behaviors and environments relate to each other and evolve over time, with the potential to improve people's productivity and wellbeing in a variety of domains. Beyond revealing population-wide patterns, the right combination of data and analysis can also help individuals identify unique characteristics of their own behavior, including conditions that could indicate the need for some form of intervention—such as an unusual increase in behaviors that signal a period of depression.\nSmartphone-based data collection comes at an appropriate time in the evolution of psychological science. Today, the field is in transition, moving away from a focus on laboratory studies with undergraduate participants towards more complex, real-world situations studied with more diverse groups of people. Smartphones offer new tools for achieving these ambitions, providing rich data about everyday behaviors in a variety of contexts.\nSo here's another way in which smartphones might transform the way we live and work: by offering insights into human psychology and behavior and, thus, supporting smarter social science."}, "questions": [{"id": "2018-12-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advances", "B) boundaries", "C) challenging", "D) currently", "E) determine", "F) devising", "G) elsewhere", "H) nevertheless", "I) otherwise", "J) predators", "K) primarily", "L) retorted", "M) spotted", "N) testimonies", "O) wrestle"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) advances\n名词\n【词性槽】主语位 + 复数动词，需复数名词\n【依据】牲畜保护领域的‘进展’\nadvances in 是固定搭配，意为‘在……领域的进展/突破’；advances in livestock\nprotection are happening with scientists painting eyes on the butts of cows 正是把这\n一‘看似疯狂、其实有创意’的尝试称作一种新进展，与首句 ‘the craziest headline’ 的吸睛口\n吻一致。\n【竞争词】N) testimonies ‘证词’与牲畜保护话题脱节\ntestimonies 是法律/宗教语境的‘证词’，无法搭配 in livestock protection 表达‘领域进展’的含\n义，整段也无证词语境。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advances", "B) boundaries", "C) challenging", "D) currently", "E) determine", "F) devising", "G) elsewhere", "H) nevertheless", "I) otherwise", "J) predators", "K) primarily", "L) retorted", "M) spotted", "N) testimonies", "O) wrestle"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) otherwise\n副词\n【词性槽】副词修饰虚拟语气情态短语\n【依据】本来就没必要发生的狮子死亡\notherwise 在此意为‘要不然、否则’，配合虚拟语气 would have been unnecessary，意思\n是‘若不是为了护畜，这些狮子的死亡本可避免’，与 overview ‘减少误杀狮子’ 的主旨一致。\n【竞争词】H) nevertheless ‘然而本可不必’逻辑不通\nnevertheless 表对前文的转折，但前文 While this makes a lot of sense 已经用 While 完成让\n步，再来一次 nevertheless 既冗余又无法配虚拟语气表达‘本可避免’。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 H) —— ‘然而’误顶虚拟前副词。While this makes a lot of sense,\nit results in many lion deaths that __(27)__ would have been unnecessary 中前半句已\n有 While 让步，nevertheless 极具迷惑性。防坑：看到 would/could/might have done\n立刻锁虚拟语气，优先想 otherwise。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advances", "B) boundaries", "C) challenging", "D) currently", "E) determine", "F) devising", "G) elsewhere", "H) nevertheless", "I) otherwise", "J) predators", "K) primarily", "L) retorted", "M) spotted", "N) testimonies", "O) wrestle"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) devising\n动词\n【词性槽】have been + Ving 并列结构\n【依据】‘设计并测试’一个方法\ndevise 意为‘设计、想出’，与 testing 构成自然搭配——研究者先‘设计’再‘测试’一种方法，\n正符合 devising and testing a method of trickery to make lions think they are being\nwatched 的科研流程。\n【竞争词】C) challenging ‘挑战并测试一个方法’语义反向\nchallenging 表‘挑战、质疑’，与 testing 并列意味着‘质疑并测试’，逻辑与本段‘研究者积极开发\n新办法’相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advances", "B) boundaries", "C) challenging", "D) currently", "E) determine", "F) devising", "G) elsewhere", "H) nevertheless", "I) otherwise", "J) predators", "K) primarily", "L) retorted", "M) spotted", "N) testimonies", "O) wrestle"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) predators\n名词\n【词性槽】and 后并列名词\n【依据】狮子 + 其他捕食者\nlions 属于捕食者，‘lions and other predators’ 是‘狮子和其他食肉动物’的常见表达。后半\n句 are far less likely to attack when they feel they are being watched 也说明主语是会\n主动捕食的动物，与 predators 完全契合。\n【竞争词】A) advances ‘进步’与狮子不同类\nadvances 是抽象名词，无法与 lions 构成同范畴并列，‘狮子和其他进步’讲不通。\nN) testimonies ‘证词’与狮子不同类\ntestimonies 是抽象法律名词，与狮子无法构成同类并列。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advances", "B) boundaries", "C) challenging", "D) currently", "E) determine", "F) devising", "G) elsewhere", "H) nevertheless", "I) otherwise", "J) predators", "K) primarily", "L) retorted", "M) spotted", "N) testimonies", "O) wrestle"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) boundaries\n名词\n【词性槽】to the __ of 介词后接名词\n【依据】人类聚居区扩张到保护区的边界\nAs conservation areas become smaller, lions are increasingly coming into contact\nwith human populations, which are expanding to the __(30)__ of these protected\nareas. 上文说保护区在缩小、狮子与人类接触增多，最自然的解释是人类活动扩张到了保护\n区的‘边界’（boundaries），与狮子接壤才接触增多。\n【竞争词】A) advances ‘进步’与地理边界不搭\nadvances 指‘进步、进展’或军事上的‘推进’，与 ‘expanding to the … of protected areas’ 的地\n理语境不匹配，扩张到保护区的‘进步’讲不通。\nJ) predators ‘捕食者’不是被扩张到达的对象\npredators 指动物个体，‘扩张到保护区的捕食者’语义违和；人类扩张应到达的是地点/边界，而\n不是一群动物。\nN) testimonies ‘证词’与本文话题完全无关\ntestimonies 多用于法庭、宗教场景，全文谈生态保护与捕猎，没有任何作证语境可以接住这\n个词。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advances", "B) boundaries", "C) challenging", "D) currently", "E) determine", "F) devising", "G) elsewhere", "H) nevertheless", "I) otherwise", "J) predators", "K) primarily", "L) retorted", "M) spotted", "N) testimonies", "O) wrestle"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) elsewhere\n副词\n【词性槽】破折号并列地点性成分\n【依据】博茨瓦纳和其他地方\nelsewhere 表‘在别处’，正好与 farmers in Botswana—and __(31)__ 中的 ‘in Botswana’\n并列，意为‘为博茨瓦纳以及其他地方的农民提供工具’，与下一句‘a low-cost, sustainable\ntool’ 的推广含义一致。\n【竞争词】D) currently 时间副词不能与地点并列\ncurrently 表时间，与 in Botswana 不属于同一逻辑维度，无法构成并列。\nH) nevertheless 转折副词不能并列地点\nnevertheless 是逻辑转折词，破折号里没有需要转折的内容。\nI) otherwise ‘否则’与地点并列不通\notherwise 表‘否则、要不然’或‘以其他方式’，无法表示地点，也无法与 in Botswana 平行。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advances", "B) boundaries", "C) challenging", "D) currently", "E) determine", "F) devising", "G) elsewhere", "H) nevertheless", "I) otherwise", "J) predators", "K) primarily", "L) retorted", "M) spotted", "N) testimonies", "O) wrestle"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) primarily\n副词\n【词性槽】be + __ + 名词短语，副词修饰\n【依据】‘主要是埋伏型猎手’最贴\nLions are __(32)__ ambush hunters, so when they feel their prey has __(33)__ them,\nthey usually give up on the hunt. 后半句说‘一旦猎物发现自己就放弃’，是对‘以埋伏为主\n要捕猎方式’的解释；primarily（主要地）与该因果链完全吻合。\n【竞争词】D) currently ‘目前是埋伏猎手’不合常识\ncurrently 强调时间‘当下’，会暗示狮子以前不是埋伏猎手，这与生物学常识及上下文都不符。\nG) elsewhere ‘在别处是埋伏猎手’语义违和\nelsewhere 表地点对比，文中并未在比较两个地点，代入读起来奇怪。\nH) nevertheless ‘然而是埋伏猎手’缺转折对象\nnevertheless 是转折副词，本句前没有可让它转折的对立语义，硬塞进来逻辑断裂。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advances", "B) boundaries", "C) challenging", "D) currently", "E) determine", "F) devising", "G) elsewhere", "H) nevertheless", "I) otherwise", "J) predators", "K) primarily", "L) retorted", "M) spotted", "N) testimonies", "O) wrestle"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) spotted\n动词\n【词性槽】has + __ + them，过去分词\n【依据】猎物‘发现’了狮子\nspot 在此意为‘看见、发现’。Lions are primarily ambush hunters, so when they feel\ntheir prey has spotted them, they usually give up on the hunt. 与第二段 lions and\nother predators are far less likely to attack when they feel they are being watched 形\n成完美互证：狮子靠埋伏，被发现就放弃。\n【竞争词】L) retorted ‘猎物反驳狮子’不合常识\nretort 指‘反驳、回嘴’，用于言语争辩场景，狮子和猎物之间不存在言语互动，代入后离题\n万里。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 L) —— ‘反驳’误填发现位。has __(33)__ them 形态层面 retorted\n完美适配，对词义不熟的考生容易硬选。防坑：跨段同义复现 ‘being watched’ →\n‘spotted’，并结合常识（狮子与猎物之间没有言语互动）一并排除。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advances", "B) boundaries", "C) challenging", "D) currently", "E) determine", "F) devising", "G) elsewhere", "H) nevertheless", "I) otherwise", "J) predators", "K) primarily", "L) retorted", "M) spotted", "N) testimonies", "O) wrestle"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) currently\n副词\n【词性槽】be + __ + 现在分词，副词修饰进行时\n【依据】研究者目前正在测试\ncurrently 表‘目前、当下’，与进行时 are testing 完美匹配，强调实验正在进行；They\nhave painted half of the cows with eyes and left the other half as normal. 表明实验已\n经设置好、正在跑数据，对应‘当下进行中’。\n【竞争词】H) nevertheless 无转折对象\n上一段并没有与本句对立的内容需要转折，nevertheless 缺乏逻辑落脚点。\nI) otherwise ‘否则正在测试’语义不通\notherwise 表‘否则/以其他方式’，代入后产生‘要不然就正在测试’这种与上下文脱节的奇怪含义。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advances", "B) boundaries", "C) challenging", "D) currently", "E) determine", "F) devising", "G) elsewhere", "H) nevertheless", "I) otherwise", "J) predators", "K) primarily", "L) retorted", "M) spotted", "N) testimonies", "O) wrestle"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) determine\n动词\n【词性槽】be able to + 动词原形\n【依据】通过追踪数据‘判定’实验是否有效\ndetermine 可带 if/whether 宾语从句，意为‘查明、判定’。Through satellite tracking of\nboth the herd and the lions in the area, they will be able to determine if their\npsychological trickery will work to help keep farmers from shooting lions. 与\noverview 中‘做实验、看效果’完全吻合。\n【竞争词】O) wrestle ‘摔跤/扭打’与科学判定不搭\nwrestle 主要表‘摔跤、扭打’或‘与难题较劲’，不能直接带 if 从句表‘判定真假’，与本句‘通过卫星\n追踪查明效果’的语境完全错位。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "It has been found that inadequate recovery often leads to poor health and accidents.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】inadequate recovery → poor health and accidents\n【定位】Research has found that there is a direct correlation between lack of recovery and increased\nincidence of health and safety problems.\n研究发现，缺乏恢复与健康和安全问题发生率上升之间存在直接关联。\n【改写】inadequate recovery ↔ lack of recovery\ninadequate ↔ lack of 的同义改写\noften leads to poor health and accidents ↔\na direct correlation between lack of recovery and increased incidence of health and safety\nproblems\n因果 ↔ 相关 + accidents ↔ safety problems\n【辨邻】 G G 段确实提到 he risks hurting everyone on the road with his impaired driving，\n看似触及「事故」，但它只是熬夜学生的个案例证，落点是 Overwork and\nexhaustion are the opposite of resilience 的 resilience 概念，而非「恢复不足与健\n康安全问题相关」的研究结论。\nWhen an exhausted student goes to school, he risks hurting everyone on the road with his\nimpaired driving", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Mental relaxation is much needed, just as physical relaxation is.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Mental relaxation 与 physical relaxation 类比\n【定位】Our brains need a rest as much as our bodies do.\n我们的大脑和身体一样，都需要休息。\n【改写】Mental relaxation is much needed ↔ Our brains need a rest\nmental ↔ brains 的「抽象 ↔ 具体器官」替换\njust as physical relaxation is ↔ as much as our bodies do\n类比连词 just as ↔ as much as 的句式转换\n【辨邻】 I I 段同样出现 brain 与 rest，但其核心论点是 rest and recovery are not the same\nthing，与陈述句「心理放松和身体放松一样必要」的类比主张不同。\nIf you lie in bed for eight hours, you may have rested, but you can still feel exhausted the next day.\nThat's because rest and recovery are not the same thing.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Adequate rest not only helps one recover, but also increases one's work efficiency.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】rest 同时 recover + raise efficiency\n【定位】Take all of your paid time off, which not only gives you recovery periods, but raises your\nproductivity and likelihood of promotion.\n把你所有的带薪假都休完，这不仅能给你恢复期，还能提升你的效率和升职可能。\n【改写】Adequate rest ↔ Take all of your paid time off\n抽象 ↔ 具体的概括关系\nhelps one recover ↔ gives you recovery periods\n动词 recover ↔ 名词 recovery periods\nincreases one's work efficiency ↔ raises your productivity\nincrease ↔ raise、efficiency ↔ productivity\n【辨邻】 D D 段说 $62 billion a year in lost productivity，是从反面讲「恢复不足导致生产力损\n失」，未正向陈述「充足休息会带来恢复 + 效率提升」的双重效应。\nlack of recovery\nK K 段聚焦 strategically stopping，给出的是 Instant/Moment、\nOfftime/Unplugged 等具体工具，并没有陈述「足够休息提升 productivity」这一双\n重效应。\nGive yourself the resources to be tough by creating internal and external recovery periods", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "The author always has a hectic time before taking a flight.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】before taking a flight 的 hectic time\n【定位】We race to get all our ground work done: packing, going through security, doing a last-minute\nwork call, calling each other, then boarding the plane.\n我们在登机前马不停蹄地把所有地面工作搞定：打包、过安检、打最后一通工作电话、彼此\n通话，然后才登机。\n【改写】hectic time ↔\nWe race to get all our ground work done: packing, going through security, doing a last-minute\nwork call, calling each other\n形容词概括 ↔ 动作清单具体化\nbefore taking a flight ↔ then boarding the plane\n时间范围对应\n【辨邻】 M M 段开篇 we've started using our plane time as a work-free zone，描述的是机\n上时段和下飞机后的恢复感，与陈述句限定的 before taking a flight 这一登机前时段\n不符。\nwe've started using our plane time as a work-free zone", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Recovery may not take place even if one seems to have stopped working.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】stopped working 但 recovery 未发生\n【定位】And just because work stops, it doesn't mean we are recovering.\n仅仅是工作停下了，并不意味着我们就在恢复。\n【改写】Recovery may not take place ↔ it doesn't mean we are recovering\nmay not ↔ doesn't mean 的含蓄否定对应\neven if one seems to have stopped working ↔ just because work stops\neven if ↔ just because 的让步对应\n【辨邻】 I I 段核心句 rest and recovery are not the same thing 谈的是「休息（如躺床）」与\n「恢复」的不等，陈述句 even if one seems to have stopped working 强调的是\n「停工」这一动作与恢复的不等，归宿应在 E 段。\nrest and recovery are not the same thing", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "It is advised that technology be used to prevent people from overworking.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】technology 用来 prevent overworking\n【定位】Amy Blankson describes how to strategically stop during the day by using technology to\ncontrol overworking.\nAmy Blankson 描述了如何利用技术控制过度工作，在白天有策略地停下来。\n【改写】It is advised that technology be used to prevent people from overworking ↔\nAmy Blankson describes how to strategically stop during the day by using technology to\ncontrol overworking\n被动建议 ↔ 主动方法 + prevent ↔ control\n【辨邻】 F F 段说 how technology extends our working hours，把 technology 视为加剧过劳\n的因素，而陈述句要求的是把 technology 当作 prevent overworking 的工具，方向\n相反。\nhow technology extends our working hours and thus interferes with necessary cognitive recovery", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Contrary to popular belief, rest does not equal recovery.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】rest does not equal recovery\n【定位】That's because rest and recovery are not the same thing.\n那是因为休息和恢复不是一回事。\n【改写】Contrary to popular belief ↔\nMost people assume that if you stop doing a task like answering emails or writing a paper,\nyour brain will naturally recover\n「反大众认知」 ↔ 「大多数人以为」的对照\nrest does not equal recovery ↔ rest and recovery are not the same thing\ndoes not equal ↔ are not the same thing 的同义否定\n【辨邻】 E E 段命题为 just because work stops, it doesn't mean we are recovering，对比对\n象是「停工 vs 恢复」；本题要求的是 rest does not equal recovery，对比对象是\n「休息 vs 恢复」。\njust because work stops, it doesn't mean we are recovering", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "The author has come to see that his problem results from a misunderstanding of the meaning of resilience.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】author 自陈：误解 resilience 才是问题源头\n【定位】Based on our current research, we have come to realize that the problem is not our hectic\nschedule or the plane travel itself; the problem comes from a misconception of what it means\nto be resilient, and the resulting impact of overworking.\n基于当前的研究，我们意识到：问题不在于繁忙的日程或飞行本身；问题在于对「具有复原\n力」含义的误解，以及由此产生的过度工作的影响。\n【改写】has come to see that his problem results from a misunderstanding of the meaning of resilience ↔\nwe have come to realize that the problem\n三组同义替换叠加\n【辨邻】 C C 段写 We often take a militaristic 与 this entire conception is scientifically\ninaccurate，描述的是大众错误观念，而陈述句要求的是「作者自己意识到」这一第一\n人称顿悟，归宿在 B 段的 we have come to realize。\nthis entire conception is scientifically inaccurate", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "People's distorted view about resilience may have developed from their upbringing.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】distorted view of resilience 源自 upbringing\n【定位】The misconception of resilience is often bred from an early age.\n对复原力的误解往往是从小被培养出来的。\n【改写】distorted view about resilience ↔ The misconception of resilience\ndistorted view ↔ misconception\ndeveloped from their upbringing ↔ bred from an early age\ndeveloped ↔ bred、upbringing ↔ from an early age\n【辨邻】 C C 段只给出 a militaristic, \"tough\" approach to resilience 这一已成型的大众错觉，\n没有追溯到 bred from an early age 这一养育起源；本题答案锁在 G 段。\na militaristic, \"tough\" approach to resilience", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "People tend to think the more determined they are, the greater their success will be.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】more determined → greater success 的大众信念\n【定位】We believe that the longer we tough it out, the tougher we are, and therefore the more\nsuccessful we will be.\n我们相信：扛得越久就越硬气，因此也就越成功。\n【改写】People tend to think ↔ We believe\ntend to think ↔ believe 的大众信念句对应\nthe more determined they are, the greater their success will be ↔\nthe longer we tough it out, the tougher we are, and therefore the more successful we will be\ndetermined ↔ tough it out / tougher，greater success ↔ more successful\n【辨邻】 B B 段说 we have come to realize that the problem 之后的归因句，这是作者反思的\n结论，而陈述句要求呈现的是大众尚未被反思的信念 the longer we tough it out, the\ntougher we are, and therefore the more successful we will be，归宿在 C 段。\nwe have come to realize that the problem", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What is the focus of the new study from Duke University?", "options": ["A) The contributors to children's early attention.", "B) The predictors of children's academic success.", "C) The factors that affect children's emotional well-being.", "D) The determinants of children's development of social skills."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题）\n【判型】题干 What is the focus of the new study 把问题指向「研究关注的是什么」，是典型的主\n旨提问。\n【拆句】What is the focus of the new study from Duke University? 是一个 wh- 疑问句，主语\nthe focus，介词短语 of the new study from Duke University 作后置定语；句子平铺直\n叙，无陷阱。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nThe contributors to children's early attention 把「早期注意力」当作被解释的对象,\n而原文是把它当作解释学业成功的「自变量」, 完全颠倒了因果关系。\nB) ✓ 正确\nearly attention skills were the most consistent predictor of academic success ↔\nThe predictors of children's academic success\nexamined early academic attention and socio\\-emotional skills and how each contributed to\nacademic success\n↔ The predictors of children's academic success\nB 项「儿童学业成功的预测因子」恰好对应预判的主线: 找出哪些早期因素能预测学业成\n功, 且能覆盖 P4 P8 等多段, 是全文焦点。\nC) 偷换概念\nThe factors that affect children's emotional well\\-being 把研究对象偷换成\nemotional well-being (情绪健康), 而原文研究的是 academic success, socio-\nemotional skills 只是其中一个因素, 不是被研究的对象。\nD) 偷换概念\nThe determinants of children's development of social skills 把研究对象说成「社交\n技能的发展」, 而原文研究的是 academic success; social acceptance 在原文只是预测\n因子, 不是被解释的目标。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "How did the researchers ensure that their findings are valid?", "options": ["A) By attaching equal importance to all possible variables examined.", "B) By collecting as many typical samples as were necessary.", "C) By preventing them from being affected by factors not under study.", "D) By focusing on the family background of the children being studied."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 How did the researchers ensure that their findings are valid? 问的是研究者具体做\n了什么，是一道明确的细节做法题。\n【拆句】How did the researchers ensure that their findings are valid? 是 how 疑问句，主句\nensure 后接 that 宾语从句 their findings are valid；结构清晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】This was after controlling for IQ, socio-economic status and academic skills at school entry.\n这是在控制了智商、社会经济地位以及入学时学业能力之后得到的结果。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文从未说研究者「同等对待所有变量」, 反而是把某些变量「控制住」以突出注意力的\n独立效应, 与 A 的「同等重视」相反。\nB) 答非所问\n原文 included 386 kindergarteners 谈的是样本数量, 而 Q47 问的是保证结论有效的方\n法; 样本量是有效性的一部分, 但原文并未把它作为本题的回答, 而是用控制变量来回应。\nC) ✓ 正确\nafter controlling for IQ, socio\\-economic status and academic skills at school entry ↔\npreventing them from being affected by factors not under study\n预判的核心是「排除其他因素干扰」, 唯一对应的就是 C 项 prevent ... from being\naffected by factors not under study, 与定位句严格同义。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文中的控制变量是 IQ / 社会经济地位 / 入学学业能力, 并不是 family background; 选\n项把控制变量偷换成「关注家庭背景」, 文中并无对应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What do we learn from the findings of the Duke study?", "options": ["A) Modest students are generally more attentive than their contemporaries.", "B) There are more children with attention difficulties than previously thought.", "C) Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder accounts for most academic failures.", "D) Children's academic performance may suffer from even slight inattention."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn from the findings of the Duke study? 问从研究发现里学到什\n么，本质是要文章里明白写出来的一条结论。\n【拆句】What do we learn from the findings of the Duke study? 是 what 疑问句，learn from\n是固定搭配「从……中得知」；句式简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】Our findings suggest that even more modest attention difficulties can increase the risk of\nnegative academic outcomes.\n我们的研究发现表明, 即使是更轻微的注意力问题, 也会增加学业上出现负面结果的风险。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n选项把 modest 当成形容人的「谦虚的」, 而原文 modest 修饰的是 attention\ndifficulties, 意为「程度较轻的」; A 项还顺势编造「谦虚的学生注意力更好」, 文中完全\n没有这个对比。\nB) 无中生有\n原文从未与「以往认知」作比较, 没有 more children than previously thought 之类的\n信息; 文章只说轻度问题也会带来风险, 而非孩子人数比之前估计的多。\nC) 过度推断\n原文 were not diagnosed with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder 明确说研究\n对象并未被诊断为 ADHD, 选项却把 ADHD 当成「大多数学业失败」的原因, 既与原文\n相反, 又把范围扩大到 most academic failures。\nD) ✓ 正确\neven more modest attention difficulties can increase the risk of negative academic outcomes ↔\nChildren's academic performance may suffer from even slight inattention\nD 项把「程度词 + 学业受损」两层都还原, 完全对应预判, 是定位句的同义改写。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does the Duke study find about children better accepted by peers?", "options": ["A) They do better academically.", "B) They are easy to get on with.", "C) They are teachers' favorites.", "D) They care less about grades."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the Duke study find about children better accepted by peers? 问关\n于某类儿童研究发现了什么，是细节题。\n【拆句】What does the Duke study find about children better accepted by peers? 是 what 疑\n问句，过去分词短语 better accepted by peers 作 children 的后置定语，相当于 children\nwho are better accepted by peers。\n【定位】Social acceptance by peers in early childhood also predicted grades in fifth grade. Children\nnot as liked by their first-grade peers had slightly lower grades in fifth grade, while those with\nhigher social acceptance had higher grades.\n童年早期获得同伴接纳也能预测五年级时的成绩。一年级时不太受欢迎的孩子在五年级成绩\n略低, 而社交接纳度更高的孩子成绩更好。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthose with higher social acceptance had higher grades ↔ They do better academically\n预判「同伴接纳度高 ⇒ 学业更好」, A 项正是这个结论的完美同义改写, 直接锁定。\nB) 无中生有\n原文谈的是「同伴接纳度↔学业成绩」, 没有谈这些孩子「好相处」与否; 选项是常识推\n测, 不是研究发现。\nC) 无中生有\n原文没有提到「老师」(teachers) 这个角色, 更没有说这些孩子是 teachers' favorites;\n完全是凭空捏造。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文明确说同伴接纳度高的孩子 had higher grades, 说明他们的成绩好, 而非「不在意\n成绩」; D 项暗示他们对成绩漠不关心, 与原文相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What can we conclude from the Duke study?", "options": ["A) Children's success is related to their learning environment.", "B) School curriculum should cover a greater variety of subjects.", "C) Social skills are playing a key role in children's development.", "D) An all-round approach should be adopted in school education."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题）\n【判型】题干 What can we conclude from the Duke study? 要求对整项研究下一个总括性结论，\n匹配主旨题特征。\n【拆句】What can we conclude from the Duke study? 是 what 疑问句，情态动词 can 表「可\n以」，from the Duke study 介词短语作 conclude 的来源；结构简单，无陷阱。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文未出现 learning environment (学习环境) 这个概念, 文章谈的是儿童自身的注意\n力、社交、自我调节等能力, 而非外部环境。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文从未谈及「课程科目种类」, 文章建议的是综合考虑各类技能 (注意力/社交/自我调\n节), 而非扩充课程科目。\nC) 以偏概全\n原文 If we neglect any of these areas, the child's development lags 强调任何一方\n面缺失都会拖累发展; 选项却把「关键作用」全部押在 social skills 一项上, 把多因素中\n的一项放大成全部, 属以偏概全。\nD) ✓ 正确\nstudent success requires a more comprehensive approach, one that incorporates not only\nacademic skills but also social, self\\-regulatory and attention skills\n↔ An all\\-round approach should be adopted in school education\nthe need to develop effective early interventions to help those with attention problems stay on\ntrack academically and for educators to encourage positive peer relationships\n↔ An all\\-round approach should be adopted in school education\nD 项 all-round approach 正是末段 comprehensive approach 的同义浓缩, 也覆盖了\nP10 P11 的多方面措施, 完全对应预判。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author say about the negative impact of smartphones?", "options": ["A) It has been overshadowed by the positive impact.", "B) It has more often than not been taken for granted.", "C) It is not so obvious but has caused some concern.", "D) It is subtle but should by no means be overstated."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about the negative impact of smartphones? 直接问作者就\n某一具体话题的说法，无 infer/imply/mainly about 等信号词。\n【拆句】题干主干 What does the author say about the negative impact of smartphones? 是\nsay about 的宾语提问，无从句无否定，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Often the advantages of convenient, mobile technology are both obvious and taken for\ngranted, leaving more subtle topics for concerned discussion: Are smartphones disturbing\nchildren's sleep? Is an inability to get away from work having a negative impact on health?\nAnd what are the implications for privacy?\n便利的移动技术所带来的好处常常既显而易见、又被视为理所当然，这就把一些更微妙的话\n题留给人们去担忧地讨论：智能手机是否干扰了孩子的睡眠？无法从工作中抽身是否对健康\n造成了负面影响？以及对隐私意味着什么？\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nIt has been overshadowed by the positive impact. 中 overshadowed by 是\"被掩\n盖、盖过\"的强烈对比关系，但原文只说正面优势被视为 taken for granted（理所当\n然），并未说它在分量上压过负面，是凭空升级的语义。\nB) 张冠李戴\nIt has more often than not been taken for granted. 把 taken for granted 这一描述\n套在 negative impact 上，但原文里 taken for granted 修饰的明明是 the\nadvantages（正面影响）；属典型的对象错配。\nC) ✓ 正确\nmore subtle topics ↔ not so obvious\nconcerned discussion ↔ caused some concern\n预判要求\"不那么明显 + 已引发担忧\"两个语义块都到位，只有 It is not so obvious but\nhas caused some concern. 同时覆盖；but 这一转折也精准对应原文的隐含让步语气。\nD) 无中生有\nIt is subtle but should by no means be overstated. 前半 subtle 对得上原文，但后半\nshould by no means be overstated（\"决不应被夸大\"）是作者本人没有表达的立场\n——原文只是把负面影响列入讨论范围，并未给出\"别夸大\"的建议。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What is considered a less obvious advantage of smartphone technology?", "options": ["A) It systematically records real human interactions.", "B) It helps people benefit from technological advances.", "C) It brings people into closer contact with each other.", "D) It greatly improves research on human behavior."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is considered a less obvious advantage of smartphone technology? 问\"什么被\n视为不那么明显的优势\"，要求复述原文给出的定义性表述。\n【拆句】题干 What is considered a less obvious advantage of smartphone technology? 中\nconsidered 是过去分词作 What 的补语，等价于 What is regarded as ...，无陷阱。\n【定位】But today, on the 10th anniversary of the iPhone, let's take a moment to consider a\nless obvious advantage: the potential for smartphone technology to revolutionize\nbehavioral science.\n但今天，在 iPhone 发布十周年之际，让我们花点时间来考虑一个不那么明显的优势：智能\n手机技术有望彻底变革行为科学。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nIt systematically records real human interactions. 把答案锁定为\"系统记录真实的人\n际互动\"，但原文 record key features of an individual's behavior and environment\n记录的是个体行为与环境，不是\"互动\"（interactions 强调人与人之间）。\nB) 答非所问\nIt helps people benefit from technological advances. 是一句几乎对任何科技都成立\n的空话，没有命中\"less obvious advantage\"所特指的\"变革行为科学\"这一具体内容。\nC) 偷换概念\nIt brings people into closer contact with each other. 把答案设定为\"让人际关系更紧\n密\"，但原文 in continuous contact with technology 说的是\"人与技术持续连接\"，并\n非\"人与人更近\"——把 contact 的对象偷换了。\nD) ✓ 正确\nrevolutionize behavioral science ↔ greatly improves research on human behavior\n预判要求落在\"变革行为研究\"这一定义上，It greatly improves research on human\nbehavior. 是四个选项里唯一同时命中\"变革程度\"与\"行为研究领域\"两个语义块的选项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What characterizes traditional psychological research?", "options": ["A) It is based on huge amounts of carefully collected data.", "B) It relies on lab observations and participants' reports.", "C) It makes use of the questionnaire method.", "D) It is often expensive and time-consuming."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What characterizes traditional psychological research? 直接问\"什么刻画了传统心理学\n研究\"，要求复述原文对其特征的客观描述。\n【拆句】题干 What characterizes traditional psychological research? 中 characterize 作动\n词\"是……的特征\"，主语是疑问代词 What，结构简洁无陷阱；考生若把 characterize 误读\n为\"性格\"则会理解偏差。\n【定位】These studies are confirming, challenging and extending what's been found using more\ntraditional approaches, in which people report how they behaved in real life or participate in\nrelatively short and artificial laboratory-based tasks.\n这些研究正在确认、挑战并拓展那些用更传统方法所得出的结论——所谓传统方法，是指让\n人们自我报告他们在现实生活中如何行事，或参加相对简短且人为设计的实验室任务。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIt is based on huge amounts of carefully collected data. 把传统研究刻画为\"基于大\n量精心采集数据\"，但原文恰恰强调传统方法是 short and artificial（短且人为）的实验\n室任务，与\"大量数据\"形成相反描述。\nB) ✓ 正确\nlaboratory-based tasks ↔ lab observations\npeople report how they behaved in real life ↔ participants' reports\n预判要求\"实验室任务 + 自我报告\"两块都到位，只有 It relies on lab observations\nand participants' reports. 把两块用 and 并列同时收齐，完全契合 in which 从句的双\n特征结构。\nC) 无中生有\nIt makes use of the questionnaire method. 把传统研究锁定为\"使用问卷法\"，但原文\n只说 report（口头/书面报告）与 laboratory-based tasks（实验室任务），从未提\nquestionnaire——这是凭空指定了一种原文没有的方法。\nD) 无中生有\nIt is often expensive and time-consuming. 给传统研究贴上\"贵且费时\"的标签，但原\n文只字未提研究的成本或时长——文中 short 修饰的是\"任务的时长\"而非\"研究的耗\n时\"，方向也不一致。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "How will future psychological studies benefit individuals?", "options": ["A) By helping them pin down their unusual behaviors.", "B) By helping them maintain a positive state of mind.", "C) By helping them live their lives in a unique way.", "D) By helping them cope with abnormal situations."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】How will future psychological studies benefit individuals? 问\"未来研究将如何使个人受\n益\"，要求复述原文对此机制的客观描述，并非要求推理。\n【拆句】题干 How will future psychological studies benefit individuals? 主干为 studies (will)\nbenefit individuals，How 提问的是方式，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Beyond revealing population-wide patterns, the right combination of data and analysis can\nalso help individuals identify unique characteristics of their own behavior, including\nconditions that could indicate the need for some form of intervention—such as an unusual\nincrease in behaviors that signal a period of depression.\n除了揭示群体层面的规律之外，恰当的数据与分析组合还能帮助个体识别自身行为的独特特\n征，包括那些可能提示需要某种形式干预的状况——例如，预示抑郁阶段的行为出现异常\n增加。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nhelp individuals identify unique characteristics of their own behavior ↔\nhelping them pin down their unusual behaviors\nan unusual increase in behaviors that signal a period of depression ↔ unusual behaviors\n预判要求\"帮个体识别 + 异常征兆\"两块都到位，By helping them pin down their\nunusual behaviors. 一句话同时覆盖：pin down 对应 identify，unusual behaviors\n同时对应 unique characteristics 与 unusual increase in behaviors。\nB) 过度推断\nBy helping them maintain a positive state of mind. 把研究的个人收益概括为\"维持\n积极心态\"，但原文 indicate the need for some form of intervention 谈的是\"识别出\n可能需要干预\"这一前置动作，并未承诺研究本身会\"帮人保持好心情\"。\nC) 偷换概念\nBy helping them live their lives in a unique way. 把 unique 这一词从\"特征\"挪到\"生\n活方式\"，原文只说识别个体行为的独特\"特征\"，与\"以独特方式生活\"没有任何关系。\nD) 过度推断\nBy helping them cope with abnormal situations. 把研究的作用升级为 cope with\n（应对、处理）异常状况，但原文 indicate the need for ... intervention 只到\"提示需\n要干预\"这一识别层面，并未说明研究本身能帮人 cope（应对）。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What do we learn about current psychological studies?", "options": ["A) They are going through a period of painful transition.", "B) They are increasingly focused on real-life situations.", "C) They are conducted in a more rigorous manner.", "D) They are mainly targeted towards undergraduates."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about current psychological studies? 问\"我们了解到当下研究怎么\n样\"，要求从原文对现状的客观描述中提取答案。\n【拆句】题干 What do we learn about current psychological studies? 主干 we learn (about)\nwhat，learn 是\"获知/了解到\"之意，结构平铺直叙无陷阱；考生若把 learn 误读为\"学习\"会\n造成理解错位。\n【定位】Today, the field is in transition, moving away from a focus on laboratory studies with\nundergraduate participants towards more complex, real-world situations studied with more\ndiverse groups of people.\n如今，这一领域正处于转型之中：正在从聚焦于以大学生为被试的实验室研究，转向以更多\n元的人群来研究更复杂的现实情境。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThey are going through a period of painful transition. 抓住 transition 原词复现做\n诱饵，但原文 in transition 仅是中性事实描述，并未给 painful（痛苦）这一情感色\n彩；painful 是凭空加上的强烈负面修饰。\nB) ✓ 正确\nmore complex, real-world situations ↔ focused on real-life situations\n预判落在 towards 之后的新方向（现实情境），They are increasingly focused on\nreal-life situations. 是四项中唯一精确对应 towards real-world situations 这一新方向\n的选项；其余选项要么说回旧方向，要么扯开话题。\nC) 无中生有\nThey are conducted in a more rigorous manner. 把转型概括为\"以更严谨方式开\n展\"，但原文谈的是\"研究对象/情境的转变\"，并未在方法严谨性上下评价。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey are mainly targeted towards undergraduates. 抓住 undergraduate 原词复\n现，但原文 moving away from a focus on laboratory studies with undergraduate\nparticipants 明确指出\"正在远离\"以大学生为被试的旧模式；选项把转出方向当成当下\n现状，方向完全反了。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2018-12-1"});
